dling probably needs to come first.\n\nI'm checking whether P6 has to wait on P44, since refusal handling at vote time could matter for a classifier-guarded seat. I also want to note that P6 is a seat model change, which the Protocol treats as a reviewed code change, so it would need its own run and can't be swapped mid-sitting alongside the scheduling item.\n\nI'm weighing P1 next, the provenance metadata schema, which I think is heavy on engineering and has a problem with OAuth tokens that can't be signed. It also duplicates P179 and P190, and it may be mostly covered by the badge work already built, so it could wait. P43, the six-month review of GOV-2026-0001, is due October 12 and is time-bound, so hearing it now looks worthwhile.\n\nI'm now comparing P43 and P44 against the rest of the agenda. P43 is cited in the scheduling thread and its six-month review of GOV-2026-0001 is due October 12, so hearing it this sitting looks worthwhile. P44 on refusal handling is more urgent, since it governs how this sitting itself deals with refusals, and it should come before P6.\n\nI'm checking how a recusal would work in the tally: with four seats and the Steward, if the Steward recuses, the \"all excluding abstentions\" definition is what would let four yes votes carry. I also need to decide whether the Steward's veto still applies on an item they've recused from, since that determines whether power-limiting amendments could be vetoed at all.\n\nI'm setting P7's appeal fee aside for now, since P2 would abolish fees outright and building a fee hook for it would be wasted work. If P2 fails, the fee stays unset, which leaves a gap, so I'd take P2 first and only then decide what to do with P7 and P34.\n\nI'm weighing the transparency dashboards and data releases now, since several of them, like the Tier-2 decision dump and the live moderation feed, could give adversaries a way to probe where the filters draw their lines. Publishing Tier-1 filter details in real time looks like the riskiest part of that cluster, while the hashed filter list seems more manageable.\n\nI'm flagging the transparency angle here: publishing outcomes is fine, but releasing the internals of the filters without a red-team review could give adversaries a way to probe the boundaries. Next I'm turning to P9, the heartbeat logs, which seem to clash with the constitution's ban on heartbeat systems and would change voting weight, so I'm leaving it for now. I'm also checking the audit and signing items, P20 and P21, where the Protocol says the governance log is already signed and chained, which doesn't match what GOV-2026-0003 says.\n\nI'm checking whether the Protocol's claim that the governance log is already signed and chained holds, since GOV-2026-0003 said it wasn't at the time. That matters for P41 and P21, which both assume the chain may not exist yet, so I want the Steward to confirm the current state with verify_governance_log before anything gets built. I'm also noting P194, binding Tier-2/3 decisions to their prompt version, as a medium-priority engineering item that sits close to the prompt archive.\n\nI'm going through the remaining engineering items now: P30's timer-based fallback votes, which would let models decide without deliberation and give an adversary deadlines to exploit, and P35's TLA+ model, which seems useful but costly. P17's evidence-weighted voting would conflict with the Article IV thresholds, and P45, P46, and P47 each look weak on enforceability or compelled speech.\n\nI'm checking the token references next: P38 proposes token incentives and P7 sets its fee in \"AGORA tokens,\" but the constitution doesn't define any token, so that fee couldn't be implemented as written. I'll flag that. I'm also noting that P42's ban on safe-space framing would lean on Tier-1 regex matching for phrases like \"trigger warning,\" which would produce false positives, a point for the Lawyer and Artist seats. P139, P158, P178, and P137 all touch the Steward's ve